Notice something important.

The CI pipeline never talks directly to Kubernetes.

Instead

Pipeline → Git

Git → ArgoCD

ArgoCD → Kubernetes

This is exactly how many production environments work.

In production, developers push code to Git, which triggers an Azure DevOps CI pipeline. The pipeline builds the application, runs tests, performs SonarQube analysis, builds a Docker image, and pushes it to Azure Container Registry. It then updates the Kubernetes manifest repository with the new image tag and commits the change to Git. ArgoCD continuously watches that repository, detects the updated manifest, compares it with the live cluster state, and automatically syncs the changes to Kubernetes using a rolling update. If someone manually changes resources in the cluster, ArgoCD detects the drift and either flags the application as OutOfSync or reconciles it back to the Git-defined state, ensuring Git remains the single source of truth.
